# V2.0

# 2026-07-24

BREAKING CHANGES

- gsutil is gone. Backups now run `gcloud storage rsync`, the tool Google recommends, because gsutil
  is removed from the Google Cloud CLI after March 2027. The gsutil component is also removed from
  the image.
- ACCESS_KEY, SECRET_KEY and BOTO_FILE are no longer supported. `gcloud storage` uses the JSON API
  and HMAC keys can only sign XML API requests, so they cannot be used at all. Authentication is now
  a service account JSON key file mounted in the container and pointed at by SERVICE_ACCOUNT_FILE,
  which is required. A container still using the removed variables stops at startup with migration
  instructions. See "Migrating from 1.x" in the README.
- The bundled boto.config file is removed with them.
- Symlinks: `gcloud storage rsync` ignores symlinks by default where gsutil followed them, so
  --no-ignore-symlinks is passed to keep the 1.x behaviour for file symlinks. One caveat: gsutil
  never followed *directory* symlinks regardless of -e, while gcloud storage may descend into them.
  If /data contains a symlink to a directory, check the first run with GCSOPTIONS=-n.

Other changes

- Updated the alpine base image from 3.18 to 3.24
- Updated the Google Cloud CLI from 396.0.0 to 577.0.0
- The Google Cloud CLI version and the target architecture (amd64 / arm64) are now build arguments
- Added timezone support through the TZ environment variable (defaults to UTC). TZ was previously
  ignored: if you already set it, the CRON_SCHEDULE now runs in that timezone instead of UTC
- Added PROJECT_ID for buckets that need a project to be set
- GCSOPTIONS keeps working: the gsutil rsync flags from 1.x (-d, -n, -C, -e, -x, and the now
  pointless -r/-R/-m) are translated to their gcloud storage equivalents, and any other value is
  passed through so gcloud storage flags can be used directly
- gcloud is on PATH, so `docker exec <container> gcloud storage ls ...` works for debugging
- CLOUDSDK_* variables set on the container are carried over to the scheduled run, so CLI settings
  such as endpoint overrides or proxy configuration apply to backups too

Reliability

- A failed sync is now both logged and reported: `run.sh backup` exits with the sync's own status, so
  cron, `docker exec` and the new healthcheck can all tell a failed backup from a successful one.
  Previously it exited 0 no matter what, and the failure was visible only by reading the log
- Added a healthcheck reporting the outcome of the last completed backup, so a backup that keeps
  failing shows up as an unhealthy container instead of only in the log. A container that has not
  completed a backup yet is healthy; disable with `docker run --no-healthcheck`
- The lock file now holds the pid of the run that took it. A lock left behind by an interrupted run
  (SIGKILL, or the container stopped mid-sync) is recognised as stale and taken over, where before it
  blocked every later backup until the container was recreated. The lock is also released when a run
  is interrupted, and two runs starting at the same moment can no longer both take it
- The backup log is trimmed to its most recent half once it passes LOG_MAX_BYTES (10 MB by default,
  0 to disable). It previously grew without bound
- crond's own messages now go to the log, so "did the schedule fire at all?" is answerable
- A CRON_SCHEDULE that is neither five fields nor an @macro is rejected at startup instead of being
  accepted by crond and then never firing
- Added RUN_ON_STARTUP=true to run one backup as the container starts, instead of waiting up to a
  whole interval to discover that the configuration is wrong. Off by default
- GCSOPTIONS is no longer expanded against the container's files: an --exclude regex such as `.*\.log`
  or `*` reaches gcloud as written. A pattern containing a space can be quoted inside the variable
  (GCSOPTIONS="-x 'my notes'"), and an unbalanced quote is reported instead of running a
  half-parsed command
- -e (--ignore-symlinks) no longer produces a contradictory pair of flags alongside the default
- run.sh is POSIX sh, matching the shell the image actually has (there is no bash in it)

Image

- About 65 MB smaller. The CLI's bundled Python is no longer shipped, since the image runs the CLI on
  Alpine's python3, and gsutil is excluded when the tarball is unpacked rather than removed
  afterwards - removing the component byte-compiled the whole CLI on its way out, which added far
  more bytecode than the files it deleted, and dropping that bytecode only moved the cost to the
  first run of every container
- The CLI tarball's SHA256 is verified during the build, so a corrupted or substituted download fails
  the build instead of being installed
- Added a .dockerignore, so the build context no longer includes .git and the docs

Testing and CI

- Added two test suites under tests/: a fast one that asserts the exact command the image builds (no
  bucket needed, runs in CI on every push) and an end-to-end one that runs real transfers against a
  local Google Cloud Storage stand-in. See tests/README.md
- CI now lints (shellcheck, hadolint) and runs the fast suite before anything is published. One case
  checks every flag the GCSOPTIONS translation can emit against `gcloud storage rsync --help`, so a
  future CLI bump that renames or drops a flag fails the build rather than the next backup
- The workflow can publish a multi-architecture image by setting the PLATFORMS variable. It still
  defaults to linux/amd64, which is all it built before; see .forgejo/workflows/README.md for what
  the runner needs first

